WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.680 --> 00:00:04.040
What church did your family go to?

2
00:00:04.040 --> 00:00:09.660
My father’s family went to Mount Zion, which is the oldest church in this area.

3
00:00:09.660 --> 00:00:15.020
And my family went to a church that was a branch of Mount Zion, which is Willow Springs.

4
00:00:15.020 --> 00:00:20.280
Mount Zion is a hundred forty plus years, and Willow Spring is a hundred five years old.

5
00:00:20.280 --> 00:00:23.060
Do you know why the church . . . Why they split off?

6
00:00:23.060 --> 00:00:31.640
Because my . . . Vincent side of the family lived in the Willow Spring area and that was . . . Dad married into the family.

7
00:00:31.640 --> 00:00:34.340
So eventually Dad migrated to Mom’s church.

8
00:00:34.340 --> 00:00:35.820
Okay.

9
00:00:35.820 --> 00:00:37.940
And who were some of the leaders in the church?

10
00:00:37.940 --> 00:00:40.940
Dallas Moss . . . Jim Johnson.

11
00:00:40.940 --> 00:00:42.320
Ervin Perkins.

12
00:00:42.320 --> 00:00:44.020
They were some of the early leaders.

13
00:00:44.020 --> 00:00:51.960
And at Willow Springs there was a Walter Smith, and Joe Vincent.

14
00:00:51.960 --> 00:01:02.040
Walter Smith and James Moss, I think, were kind of wealthy at that particular day and time in standing.

15
00:01:02.040 --> 00:01:08.320
Walter Smith had a sugarcane mill, and Jim Moss apparently had a mill or something that he ran,

16
00:01:08.320 --> 00:01:12.620
and then later the post office that he kind of sublet to someone else.

17
00:01:12.620 --> 00:01:15.800
We didn’t know that term at the time, but that was what was going on.

18
00:01:15.800 --> 00:01:18.180
What happened to the mills?

19
00:01:18.180 --> 00:01:21.300
Eventually I guess they just closed down with modernization.

20
00:01:21.300 --> 00:01:23.000
Times changed.

21
00:01:23.000 --> 00:01:25.380
Like what year . . . Like what timeframe was that?

22
00:01:25.380 --> 00:01:34.560
The sugarcane mill that Walter Smith operated phased out probably when I was about four or five years old.

23
00:01:34.560 --> 00:01:37.040
And the reason I say that was,

24
00:01:37.040 --> 00:01:47.220
he kind of became like my pal because I was the oldest grandchild on that side of the family and sometimes he would take me to go to what was called his branding pen,

25
00:01:47.220 --> 00:01:51.640
and brand cows and feed his horses and whatever.

26
00:01:51.640 --> 00:01:54.580
And I remember walking along his old mill.

27
00:01:54.580 --> 00:02:02.920
And then on this end of the retrospect, I can more or less remember just church services and whatever.

28
00:02:02.920 --> 00:02:11.440
But there was an old, old resident called Pete Moss at the time that lived in what we would call today a shanty.

29
00:02:11.440 --> 00:02:13.860
And I guess he was very happy and content.

30
00:02:13.860 --> 00:02:15.020
He had a lot of dogs.

31
00:02:15.020 --> 00:02:16.720
His wife name was Virginia.

32
00:02:16.720 --> 00:02:19.380
And he would travel back and forth by wagon.

33
00:02:19.380 --> 00:02:22.320
And I was just a little girl at that particular time.

34
00:02:22.320 --> 00:02:25.780
Probably twelve years old at the oldest.

35
00:02:25.780 --> 00:02:27.780
Twelve, thirteen at the most, see.

36
00:02:27.780 --> 00:02:31.500
And it just so happened that the property that my home is sitting on,

37
00:02:31.500 --> 00:02:36.000
I purchased that from, I think his brother, or one at least of his ancestors.
